# Committees, Commissions and Reports: Who Studied What

> India has a habit of turning hard questions into commissions: how should power be shared with states, how should
> schools be organised, who deserves reservation? The reports often shaped real laws and institutions. Exam papers
> test the match between the name, the subject, the year and the headline recommendation.

## 1. Why Committees Matter and How to Study Them

A **committee** or **commission** is a body set up by the government (or Parliament) to study an issue and recommend action. Its report is advisory: the government may accept, modify or ignore it. A body set up under Article 340 for backward classes, or under the Commissions of Inquiry Act, 1952 for an inquiry, has a legal origin; most expert committees are set up by executive order.

[definition]
A **Commission of Inquiry** is set up under the Commissions of Inquiry Act, 1952 to inquire into a matter of public importance. Its recommendations are advisory, but the government must table the report in Parliament with an action-taken memorandum.
[/definition]

Study each item with a quick three-part tag: **name, subject, signature recommendation**. A fourth tag, the chairperson, helps because many commissions carry their chairman's name (Mandal, Sarkaria, Kothari).

## 2. Centre-State Relations and Federalism

| Body | Year set up | Subject | Signature point |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Rajamannar Committee | 1969 | Centre-state relations (Tamil Nadu government) | Argued for greater state autonomy |
| Sarkaria Commission | 1983 (report 1988) | Centre-state relations | Chair was Justice R.S. Sarkaria; recommended a permanent Inter-State Council and keeping a strong Centre |
| Punchhi Commission | 2007 (report 2010) | Centre-state relations | Chair was Justice M.M. Punchhi; revisited the Sarkaria findings |
| Dhar Commission | 1948 | Linguistic reorganisation of provinces | Preferred administrative convenience over language |
| States Reorganisation Commission (Fazl Ali) | 1953 (report 1955) | Reorganising states | Members were Fazl Ali, K.M. Panikkar and H.N. Kunzru; led to the States Reorganisation Act, 1956 |

The Inter-State Council under Article 263 was set up in 1990 after the Sarkaria recommendation. Keep Sarkaria (1983) and Punchhi (2007) apart: both studied centre-state relations, but a generation apart.

## 3. Local Government and Administration

| Body | Year | Subject | Signature point |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Balwant Rai Mehta Committee | 1957 | Community Development and rural bodies | Recommended a **three-tier** Panchayati Raj: Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti, Zila Parishad |
| Ashok Mehta Committee | 1977 | Panchayati Raj revival | Recommended a **two-tier** system: Zila Parishad and Mandal Panchayat |
| L.M. Singhvi Committee | 1986 | Panchayati Raj | Urged constitutional status for local government |
| First Administrative Reforms Commission | 1966 | Administration | Recommended Lokpal and Lokayukta institutions |
| Second Administrative Reforms Commission | 2005 | Administration | Veerappa Moily led it in its early phase |

Rajasthan started Panchayati Raj on 2 October 1959 at Nagaur, following the Balwant Rai Mehta report. Constitutional status came much later through the 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992).

Memory trick: **B**alwant Rai Mehta has **3** words in "three-tier"; **A**shok Mehta had **2** tiers. B3, A2.

## 4. Education Commissions

| Commission | Year | Level studied | Signature point |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hunter Commission | 1882 | Education under British rule | Emphasised primary and secondary education |
| Radhakrishnan Commission | 1948 | University education | First commission of independent India on higher education |
| Mudaliar Commission | 1952 | Secondary education | Reviewed secondary schooling |
| Kothari Commission | 1964 (report 1966) | Entire education system | Backed the 10+2+3 pattern and the three-language formula |

Kothari is also known as the Education Commission, led by D.S. Kothari, the scientist. Do not mix it with the 1948 University Education Commission, led by S. Radhakrishnan.

## 5. Social Justice, Economy and Governance

| Body | Year | Subject | Signature point |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mandal Commission | 1979 (report 1980) | Socially and educationally backward classes | Led by B.P. Mandal; recommended 27 per cent reservation for OBCs |
| Sachar Committee | 2005 (report 2006) | Socio-economic condition of Muslims | Chaired by Justice Rajinder Sachar |
| Santhanam Committee | 1962 | Corruption in administration | Led to the Central Vigilance Commission being set up in 1964 |
| Swaran Singh Committee | 1976 | Constitutional changes | Its recommendations led to the Fundamental Duties in the 42nd Amendment |
| Shah Commission | 1977 | Excesses during the Emergency | Justice J.C. Shah investigated |
| Liberhan Commission | 1992 | Babri Masjid demolition | Justice M.S. Liberhan headed it |
| Malimath Committee | 2000 | Criminal justice reform | Reported in 2003 |
| Verma Committee | 2012 | Laws on sexual crimes | Reported in 2013 |
| Narasimham Committee | 1991 | Financial and banking sector reforms | Banking reform blueprint after liberalisation |
| Tendulkar Committee | 2005 | Estimation of poverty | Reported in 2009 |

[example]
A question reads: "Which commission recommended 27 per cent reservation for OBCs in central posts?" The answer is the Mandal Commission. Distractors will include Kothari (education) and Sarkaria (federalism). The Mandal recommendation was implemented by the V.P. Singh government in 1990, and the Supreme Court upheld it in the Indra Sawhney case in 1992 while setting a ceiling of 50 per cent on reservations.
[/example]

## 6. Pre-Independence Missions Often Grouped With Them

| Body | Year | Why it matters |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Simon Commission | 1927 | All-white commission to review the working of the 1919 Act; met with the "Simon Go Back" protests |
| Nehru Report | 1928 | Indian leaders' answer to the challenge to draft a constitution |
| Cabinet Mission | 1946 | Three British Cabinet ministers sent with a plan for transfer of power; led to the Constituent Assembly elections |

The Simon Commission was named after its chairman, Sir John Simon, and had no Indian member, which is why Indians boycotted it. This fact is a regular one-liner in papers.

## 7. Rapid Revision Tricks

- **Panchayat pair**: Balwant Rai (1957, three-tier); Ashok Mehta (1977, two-tier).
- **Federal pair**: Sarkaria (1983) first; Punchhi (2007) second.
- **Education ladder**: Radhakrishnan for universities (1948), Mudaliar for secondary (1952), Kothari for all levels (1964).
- **Emergency**: Shah Commission examined excesses of the Emergency.
- **Corruption**: Santhanam gave the CVC.
- Hunter (1882) in education is different from the 1919 Hunter Committee on the Jallianwala Bagh massacre. Check the year in the question.

[summary]
Committees and commissions are tagged by name, year, subject and a signature recommendation. Balwant Rai Mehta (1957) proposed three-tier Panchayati Raj; Ashok Mehta (1977) a two-tier model. Sarkaria (1983) and Punchhi (2007) studied centre-state relations. Mandal (1979) recommended 27 per cent OBC reservation. Kothari (1964) shaped the 10+2+3 pattern, Radhakrishnan (1948) covered universities and Mudaliar (1952) secondary education. Santhanam led to the CVC, Swaran Singh to Fundamental Duties, Shah examined the Emergency, Sachar studied Muslims, and the States Reorganisation Commission led to the 1956 Act.
[/summary]
